- How would you extend to 128x128 interleaving into 256 bits?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of bit-twiddling tricks for 128-bit values without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR/popcount for 64-bit chunks
- Safe 128-bit aggregation without branches

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  auto count64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v>>8);
    v = v + (v>>16);
    v = v + (v>>32);
    return (uint64_t)(v & 0x7F);
  };
  uint64_t c1 = count64(hi);
  uint64_t c2 = count64(lo);
  return (uint32_t)(c1 + c2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit value?
- What are the risks of using this in performance-critical hot paths compared to builtin intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128 bit manipulations and zero edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit tricks
- 128 bit layout as hi and lo
- De Bruijn based ctz for 64 and 32 bit values
- Handling zero input gracefully

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline int ctz64(uint64_t x) {
  if (x == 0) return 64;
  static const int tab[64] = { /* 64 entries */ };
  uint64_t y = x & -x;
  return tab[(y * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ULL) >> 58];
}

int lsbIndex128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  int a = ctz64(lo);
  int b = 64 + ctz64(hi);
  int m = - (int)(lo != 0);
  int res = (a & m) | (b & ~m);
  return (lo | hi) == 0 ? -1 : res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if 128 bit intrinsics are unavailable
- Extend to msbIndex128 with branchless patterns

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses proficiency in low-level bit manipulation using only shifts and masks—skills essential for systems programming, embedded development, and cryptographic code. It demonstrates understanding of parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR/SIMD Within A Register) techniques.

## Key Concepts
- **Bit masking and shifting:** Extracting and repositioning specific bit ranges
- **Parallel processing:** Operating on multiple data elements (bytes) simultaneously via mask patterns
- **Byte-local vs. global reversal:** This approach reverses bits within each byte independently, unlike global bit-reversal algorithms
- **Magic constants:** The hexadecimal masks (`0xF0F0F0F0`, `0xCCCCCCCC`, `0xAAAAAAAA`) are standard patterns for these swaps

## Complete Code Example
```c
uint32 reverseBitsInEachByte32(uint32 x) {
  // Step 1: Swap adjacent nibbles (0xF0 = 11110000 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xF0F0F0F0) >> 4) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);

  // Step 2: Swap adjacent 2-bit pairs (0xCC = 11001100 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xCCCCCCCC) >> 2) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);

  // Step 3: Swap adjacent individual bits (0xAA = 10101010 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xAAAAAAAA) >> 1) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);

  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compose 128-bit reversal from 64-bit primitives without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit reversal algorithm
- Cross-block mapping for 128-bit reversal
- Constant-time, no-branch implementation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo_rev = rev64(x.lo);
  uint64_t hi_rev = rev64(x.hi);
  return (uint128){ .hi = lo_rev, .lo = hi_rev };
}
```

